cluster.content:
In Section 6, the ILPA Due Diligence Questionnaire 2.0 asks about two related transaction types over the last five years:

1. GP-led secondaries
2. Continuation funds

For both, the questionnaire first asks whether the firm has completed any such transactions in that period. If the answer is yes, it then asks transaction-by-transaction questions centered on governance, fairness, and LP treatment.

For GP-led secondary transactions, Section 6 asks whether:
- conflicts were reviewed by the LPAC;
- existing LPs were given a status quo option;
- GP carry was rolled over at 100%;
- portfolio companies that were in IPO or pre-IPO discussions were excluded.

For continuation fund transactions, it asks the same core points:
- whether conflicts were reviewed by the LPAC;
- whether existing LPs were given a status quo option;
- whether GP carry was rolled over at 100%;
- whether portfolio companies in IPO or pre-IPO discussions were excluded.

Section 6 also asks the firm to describe its conflict review process for GP-led secondaries and continuation funds, including:
- how and when conflicts are reviewed;
- how the LPAC and LPs are involved;
- what information is shared to support the review;
- how the review process is structured;
- how any benefits received by the firm that do not flow to LPs are handled;
- how transparency is provided if LPAC members or firm affiliates participate as acquirers.

The evidences also indicate that the questionnaire may seek added detail on transaction execution and disclosure, such as:
- whether IPO/pre-IPO companies were included or excluded;
- how economics were split between the GP and the acquirer;
- how valuation was determined, including modeling assumptions;
- whether an independent fairness opinion was obtained;
- how the bidding universe was identified;
- whether a secondary manager was involved;
- who was included in the acquirer pool;
- an overview of bids received;
- why certain acquirers may have been excluded;
- how much time LPs had to decide whether to sell, exit, or roll over;
- whether LPs had an option not to roll over;
- the stage of the fund at the time of the transaction;
- the age of the assets involved;
- the quality and outlook of remaining investments;
- expected time to realization for remaining investments.

Overall, Section 6 is designed to assess whether GP-led secondaries and continuation fund transactions were handled with proper conflict management, meaningful LP choice, economic alignment, and robust transparency.
